# Processing Strategy

The analysis employs a **two-stage aggregation strategy** to optimize memory usage and computational efficiency. Data is processed in **decade-long chunks (10-year groups)** to manage memory constraints.

## Stage A: Daily → Monthly Maximum

This is the **computationally intensive step**, processing up to 31 daily layers per month.

```{r stage-a, eval=FALSE}
library(terra)
library(lubridate)

# Pseudocode for Stage A
# For each decade chunk:
#   1. Open daily NetCDF as SpatRaster
#   2. Extract time index
#   3. Group layers by year-month
#   4. Compute pixel-wise max across each month
#   5. Write monthly max raster stack to disk

daily_to_monthly_max <- function(daily_nc, out_path) {
  r       <- terra::rast(daily_nc)
  dates   <- terra::time(r)
  ym      <- format(dates, "%Y-%m")
  monthly <- terra::tapp(r, index = ym, fun = "max", na.rm = TRUE)
  terra::writeRaster(monthly, out_path, overwrite = TRUE)
  monthly
}
```

## Stage B: Monthly → Yearly Maximum

This step is **extremely fast** because it only processes 12 monthly layers per year instead of 365 daily layers.

```{r stage-b, eval=FALSE}
monthly_to_yearly_max <- function(monthly_rast, out_path) {
  yr     <- substr(names(monthly_rast), 1, 4)   # or use terra::time()
  yearly <- terra::tapp(monthly_rast, index = yr, fun = "max", na.rm = TRUE)
  terra::writeRaster(yearly, out_path, overwrite = TRUE)
  yearly
}
```

## Decade Chunking

```{r decades, eval=FALSE}
decades <- list(
  c(1945, 1954),
  c(1955, 1964),
  c(1965, 1974),
  c(1975, 1984),
  c(1985, 1994),
  c(1995, 2004),
  c(2005, 2012)   # final chunk is 8 years
)

for (d in decades) {
  message("Processing decade: ", d[1], "–", d[2])
  # 1. Subset NetCDF to decade range
  # 2. Run Stage A (daily -> monthly max)
  # 3. Append result to a master monthly stack
}

# After all decades:
# 4. Run Stage B on the master monthly stack
```

